/**
 * The mark a row led to by a reference of the broken-links warning carries while the eye finds it.
 * Both panels wear the same one: the rule of the accent is written once, next to the warning itself
 * (`shared/ui/broken-links-notice/style.css`), and the row of a field is the same row on both sides.
 */
export const BROKEN_LINK_HIGHLIGHT_CLASS = '--broken-link-target';

/**
 * Same length as the `2.5s` of the `--broken-link-target` accent in the stylesheet: the class is taken
 * off exactly when its animation ends. Changing one side alone leaves the accent either cut short or
 * stuck on the row, so both records name each other.
 */
export const BROKEN_LINK_HIGHLIGHT_DURATION = 2500;

/**
 * How long the panel waits for the form of a construction to report itself before it settles for the
 * card of that construction. The wait ends at one of three answers the panel already has - the form
 * registered, the form left the screen, the loading failed - and this deadline is only for the fourth
 * case, where none of them ever comes.
 *
 * Measured 2026-09-17 on the two forms the approach can reach, seven cold presses each, from the press
 * to the row of the field being marked (`chart.bundle` on a local stand, one worker):
 *  - `Task2Activity` inside `TasksComplexActivity`, 43 controls, the heaviest dialog that comes as
 *    ready HTML - 1797 / 2100 / 3059 ms (min / median / max);
 *  - the relation form of `CrmGetConversionDataActivity`, the branch the server describes by controls -
 *    2283 / 2706 / 2765 ms.
 * Three times the worst of those, rounded: a stand three times slower than the measured one still ends
 * at the field. The previous 3000 ms sat inside that very spread, so the first press on a construction
 * ended at the card as often as not.
 *
 * Not longer than that, and the reason is what the deadline costs. A press is answered for the eye at
 * once - the card is scrolled to, focused and ringed before any waiting starts - so what the deadline
 * holds back is the spoken outcome alone. On a machine loaded to saturation the same presses took up
 * to 13,2 s, and covering that band would turn a form that never answers into thirteen seconds of
 * silence for whoever listens rather than looks. Such a load belongs to a test run of several browsers
 * at once and not to a portal, so it is deliberately left outside.
 *
 * Longer than the accent above either way, so an outcome never lands while a mark already set is still
 * burning.
 *
 * The deadline is a regular path and not only the case of a form that never answered: a render that
 * was cancelled, a property dialog that answered with nothing, a form that never reached its own
 * point of readiness - all of them end here.
 */
export const BROKEN_LINK_FORM_WAIT_TIMEOUT = 10000;

/**
 * How long the row of a field an approach ended at may take its focus back from the form it stands
 * in. A form that arrives after the press is still mounting when it registers itself
 * (`setRenderedForm`), and the approach puts the focus on the row in the middle of that mounting -
 * so the tail of the mounting overrides it. The legacy dialog does exactly that on a timer of its
 * own: `setTimeout(() => table.querySelector('#bpastitle')?.focus(), 100)` in
 * `bizprocdesigner/admin/bizproc_activity_settings.php`, the title control of the action.
 *
 * Measured 2026-09-17 on the cold path of `Task2Activity`: the row took the focus at 7530 ms and
 * `textarea#bpastitle` took it away at 7586 - 56 ms later, the remainder of that 100 ms timer.
 * Three times the timer of the dialog and five times the measured theft, which leaves room for a
 * slower stand while staying far short of the accent above: the window is closed long before the
 * mark itself comes off the row.
 *
 * A window and not a guard standing all the time: what is worth taking back is the focus of the
 * very mounting the approach was waiting for, and nothing later. Whoever moves the focus themselves
 * keeps it, whenever they do it.
 */
export const BROKEN_LINK_FOCUS_RECAPTURE_WINDOW = 300;
